#pragma once


#include "peano4/RunMultipleSteps.h"
#include "tarch/multicore/orchestration/Strategy.h"

#include <functional>
#include <set>


namespace exahype2 {
  /**
   * Configure the runtime of an ExaHyPE 2 simulation
   *
   * The main() generated by exahype2.api.generators.DefaultSimulationMain
   * calls this routine once the parallel environment, the data types and
   * Peano's singletons are initialised, and before it sets the load
   * balancing. The routine
   *
   * - sets the deadlock time out of tarch::mpi::Rank (if timeout>=0),
   * - configures the number of threads (tarch::multicore::Core) and the
   *   GPUs (tarch::accelerator::Device),
   * - installs the multicore orchestration, and
   * - checks that MPI runs use more than one thread, as MPI runs without
   *   multithreading are not supported.
   *
   * @param timeout Deadlock time out in seconds. A negative value keeps the
   *   default.
   * @param numberOfThreads Number of threads, or
   *   tarch::multicore::Core::UseDefaultNumberOfThreads.
   * @param gpus Devices to use. Empty if no GPU is to be used.
   * @param orchestration Multicore orchestration. The routine hands it over
   *   to tarch::multicore::setOrchestration(), which takes ownership.
   * @return false if the configuration is invalid, i.e. if this is an MPI
   *   run without multithreading. main() then has to terminate.
   */
  bool configureRuntime(
    int                                        timeout,
    int                                        numberOfThreads,
    const std::set<int>&                       gpus,
    tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy* orchestration
  );

  /**
   * Run an ExaHyPE 2 simulation
   *
   * Every ExaHyPE 2 project consists of four runners: one setting up the
   * simulation (mesh construction and initial conditions), one plotting the
   * solution, one running a time step, and one adapting the mesh and
   * rebalancing the load between two time steps. The routine realises the
   * main loop over these runners:
   *
   * - The global master runs setupSimulation once. Afterwards, it
   *   alternates between plotting and time stepping as long as the minimum
   *   time stamp has not reached minTerminalTime, the maximum time stamp has
   *   not reached maxTerminalTime, and the maximum time step size is
   *   positive. A plot is only triggered if wantsToPlot() holds, and never
   *   twice in a row, as a plot does not advance the simulation. Once the
   *   loop has terminated, the master plots a final time if wantsToPlot()
   *   holds.
   * - Right after each time step, the global master runs meshAdaptation if
   *   isMeshAdaptationRequired() holds. It never runs meshAdaptation if
   *   this runner has no mesh sweeps at all, i.e. if no solver has
   *   contributed to the mesh adaptation phase.
   * - After each time step, the global master checks whether the minimum
   *   and the maximum time stamp have changed. If neither has changed, it
   *   writes a warning, as a single step might legitimately not advance in
   *   time (a solver might for example reject and rerun a step). If they
   *   do not change in the subsequent time step either, the simulation
   *   is stuck. The master writes an error and leaves the loop as if it had
   *   reached the terminal time, i.e. it plots a final time if
   *   wantsToPlot() holds and then terminates regularly. Any time step in
   *   which a time stamp changes resets this check.
   * - All other ranks (Peano workers) run whatever program step the master
   *   has selected until the master tells them to terminate.
   *
   * Each runner which has run is reported to report, and report's final
   * results are written once the loop has terminated. With OpenMP, the
   * whole loop runs within the master thread of a parallel region, so
   * Peano can spawn tasks.
   *
   * The routine replaces the main loop which
   * exahype2.api.generators.DefaultSimulationMain used to generate. The
   * time stamps and step sizes stem from the project's solver repository,
   * so the generated main() hands them over as functors.
   *
   * @param setupSimulation Runner setting up the simulation.
   * @param plot Runner plotting the solution.
   * @param timeStep Runner running one time step.
   * @param meshAdaptation Runner adapting the mesh and rebalancing the load.
   *   Its getFirstSubstep() is peano4::parallel::Node::UndefProgramStep if
   *   it has no mesh sweeps.
   * @param report Report to which each run of a runner is reported.
   * @param wantsToPlot Does any solver want to plot?
   * @param getMinTimeStamp Minimum time stamp over all solvers.
   * @param getMaxTimeStamp Maximum time stamp over all solvers.
   * @param getMaxTimeStepSize Maximum time step size over all solvers.
   * @param minTerminalTime The simulation terminates once the minimum time
   *   stamp reaches this value.
   * @param maxTerminalTime The simulation terminates once the maximum time
   *   stamp reaches this value.
   */
  void runSimulation(
    peano4::RunMultipleSteps&         setupSimulation,
    peano4::RunMultipleSteps&         plot,
    peano4::RunMultipleSteps&         timeStep,
    peano4::RunMultipleSteps&         meshAdaptation,
    peano4::RunMultipleSteps::Report& report,
    std::function<bool()>             wantsToPlot,
    std::function<double()>           getMinTimeStamp,
    std::function<double()>           getMaxTimeStamp,
    std::function<double()>           getMaxTimeStepSize,
    double                            minTerminalTime,
    double                            maxTerminalTime
  );

  /**
   * Does the mesh have to be adapted or the load to be rebalanced?
   *
   * runSimulation() asks this predicate right after each time step. It
   * holds if
   *
   * - ::exahype2::RefinementControlService holds committed refinement or
   *   erase events, i.e. a time step has asked for a mesh change which
   *   nobody has realised yet, or
   * - a load balancing strategy is installed and has split recently
   *   (toolbox::loadbalancing::AbstractLoadBalancing::hasSplitRecently()),
   *   i.e. it has triggered a rebalancing which Peano still has to carry
   *   out.
   *
   * These are the same predicates which the repeat conditions of the mesh
   * construction use. As the predicate is evaluated on the global master
   * only, both refer to this rank's view of the simulation.
   */
  bool isMeshAdaptationRequired();

  /**
   * Shut down the runtime of an ExaHyPE 2 simulation
   *
   * Counterpart of configureRuntime(), plus the shutdown of everything
   * main() has initialised: the routine writes the statistics, shuts down
   * the load balancing, Peano's singletons, the project's data types,
   * SmartMPI, the non-critical assertions, and finally the parallel
   * environment.
   *
   * @param shutdownDatatypes Shuts down the project's data types, i.e.
   *   typically the project's
   * repositories::DataRepository::shutdownDatatypes(). It is called after
   * Peano's singletons are down and before SmartMPI and the parallel
   * environment are shut down.
   */
  void shutdownRuntime(std::function<void()> shutdownDatatypes);
} // namespace exahype2
